<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wetterau</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wetterau is a fertile lowland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vogelsberg">Vogelsberg</a> north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, named after the river Wetter. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> enclosed it within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limes-germanicus">Limes Germanicus</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a land of imperial cities such as Friedberg and of comital houses such as Isenburg, Hanau and Solms, which formed the Wetterau Association of counts. It remains an important farming region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wetterau.jpg" alt="Wetterau" width="320" height="85" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MdE · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wetterau_W-E.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Wetterau is a fertile, gently rolling tract on the eastern side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taunus">Taunus</a> and south-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vogelsberg">Vogelsberg</a>, between the hill country of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hesse">Oberhessen</a> and the Taunus mountains. Its name, meaning Wetter meadow, comes from the small Wetter, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidda">Nidda</a>, but the region is crossed by several other streams and rivers, among them the Nidda, the Nidder, the Horloff and the Usa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>One of the oldest cultural landscapes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the Wetterau was settled from the Neolithic Age onwards; finds include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> tombs and the Glauberg, a site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> it was of high strategic value in the advance into free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, and after the Gallic wars of 58 to 51 BCE forts and roads were built there, the fertile land being ringed by fortifications of the limes. It is first named in 779 in the Codex Aureus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch">Lorsch</a>.</p><p>From the time of Emperor Frederick I its towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> am Main, Wetzlar, Gelnhausen and Friedberg were promoted as royal bases and centres of royal rights over trade and justice. The Lords of Hagen-Münzenberg minted coin, and from the 13th century the towns' market rights grew into regular fairs at Frankfurt and Friedberg. When the Münzenberg family died out in 1255, powerful families such as those of Hanau, Eppstein and Isenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budingen">Büdingen</a> came to the fore.</p><p>From 1422 the knights, lords and counts of the Wetterau formed alliances that gave the region a shared identity. Its order at the turn to modern times rested on four imperial cities, of which in the end only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> mattered, on the Wetterau knighthood gathered at the imperial castle of Friedberg, on twenty comital lineages, and on towns held in joint ownership, such as Friedberg, Kronberg and Gelnhausen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Most of the region lies in the district of Wetteraukreis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. Many of its archaeological finds are shown in the Wetterau-Museum in Friedberg. The Frankfurt Trade Fair continues the tradition of its medieval fairs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wetterau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A study of Vráble found that fields were used as pastures to make manure, and a GIS study of the <b>Wetterau</b> concluded that grazing land, not arable land, limited settlement there.</blockquote><a href="/culture/linear-pottery-culture">Linear Pottery culture · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hohenstaufen built Friedberg castle in the <b>Wetterau</b> in the 12th century and garrisoned it with castle men, imperial knights who lived in the fortress.</blockquote><a href="/state/burgraviate-of-friedberg">Burgraviate of Friedberg</a></li><li><blockquote>Domitian fought the Chatti in 83 to 85 and pushed the frontier forward along the Taunus and <b>Wetterau</b>, the line later fortified as the Limes.</blockquote><a href="/state/chatti">Chatti</a></li><li><blockquote>The lords of Hanau, raised to counts in 1429, ruled lands around Hanau on the Main and had inherited part of the Münzenberg lordship in the <b>Wetterau</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-hanau-munzenberg">County of Hanau-Münzenberg</a></li><li><blockquote>The house of Isenburg held lands in the <b>Wetterau</b> and around Büdingen from the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-isenburg-budingen">County of Isenburg-Büdingen</a></li><li><blockquote>Through marriage they also acquired the County of Nidda in the <b>Wetterau</b> in the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ziegenhain">County of Ziegenhain</a></li></ul></section>
